## Proof

**Claim.** There exists a bijection $f:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ whose graph $\Gamma(f)=\{(x,f(x)):x\in[0,1]\}$ is dense in $[0,1]\times[0,1]$.

**Construction.**

Let $\mathcal{B}=\{W_1,W_2,\dots\}$ be the countable collection of all open rectangles in $[0,1]^2$ with rational endpoints, i.e. $W_k=(a_k,b_k)\times(c_k,d_k)$ with $a_k,b_k,c_k,d_k\in\mathbb{Q}\cap[0,1]$. This is a base for the topology on $[0,1]^2$, so a set is dense iff it meets every $W_k$.

We build a partial injective function $g$ on a countable subset $S\subset[0,1]$ by induction. At step $k$, suppose $x_1,\dots,x_{k-1}$ and values $g(x_1),\dots,g(x_{k-1})$ have been chosen. Since $(a_k,b_k)\cap\mathbb{Q}$ is infinite, pick
$$x_k\in (a_k,b_k)\cap\mathbb{Q}\setminus\{x_1,\dots,x_{k-1}\}.$$
Since $(c_k,d_k)\cap\mathbb{Q}$ is infinite, pick
$$g(x_k)\in (c_k,d_k)\cap\mathbb{Q}\setminus\{g(x_1),\dots,g(x_{k-1)\}}.$$
This is always possible. Set $S=\{x_k:k\geq 1\}$; then $g:S\to g(S)$ is injective by construction, and for every $k$,
$$(x_k,g(x_k))\in W_k.\tag{$\ast$}$$

**Density of the partial graph.** By $(\ast)$, the graph $\Gamma(g)=\{(x_k,g(x_k)):k\geq 1\}$ meets every basic rectangle $W_k$, hence is dense in $[0,1]^2$.

**Extension to a bijection.** The set $S$ is countable, so $[0,1]\setminus S$ has cardinality $\mathfrak{c}$ (continuum). Likewise $g(S)$ is countable, so $[0,1]\setminus g(S)$ has cardinality $\mathfrak{c}$. Hence there exists a bijection
$$h:[0,1]\setminus S\;\to\;[0,1]\setminus g(S).$$
Define $f=g\cup h$, i.e.
$$f(x)=\begin{cases}g(x)&x\in S,\\ h(x)&x\in[0,1]\setminus S.\end{cases}$$

**$f$ is a bijection.** The domains $S$ and $[0,1]\setminus S$ partition $[0,1]$; the ranges $g(S)$ and $[0,1]\setminus g(S)$ partition $[0,1]$. Since $g:S\to g(S)$ is injective and $h:[0,1]\setminus S\to[0,1]\setminus g(S)$ is a bijection, $f$ is a bijection $[0,1]\to[0,1]$.

**$f$ has dense graph.** Since $\Gamma(g)\subseteq\Gamma(f)$ and $\Gamma(g)$ is dense in $[0,1]^2$, its superset $\Gamma(f)$ is also dense.

Therefore such a bijective function exists. $\blacksquare$

